  "tour": {
    "title": "John’s New Creation: Following the Genesis Echoes",
    "intro": "This tour starts with John’s opening “in the beginning,” then follows the Genesis echoes through his prologue, his sevens and eighth-day resurrection, his water and Eden imagery, and the garden scene of John 20. The clips build a case for what John means by new creation rather than treating it as a detached slogan.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:4613377c-3e5d-11f1-b7fb-27eb7613d571",
        "startSec": 360,
        "endSec": 690,
        "why": "This lays the foundation directly from John 1. The speakers say John “begins his gospel with Eden, with new creation,” and trace the darkness, light, water, Spirit, and Adam imagery to Jesus as the “new Adam,” who remakes and heals the fallen humanity. Hear this first for the basic meaning of the Genesis allusion.",
        "episodeTitle": "John 1: In the Beginning Was the Word",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG8007534722.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3919,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:beginning",
        "startSec": 1235,
        "endSec": 1470,
        "why": "This moves from imagery to literary structure. The hosts argue that John’s prologue has “exactly the literary design” of Genesis 1—an introduction, “two sequences of three,” and a concluding section—and connect its unfinished shape with Jesus’ death, empty tomb, and “new creation made complete.”",
        "episodeTitle": "In the Beginning – John 1",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/8f0c5975-65cf-4a02-baf0-786ba6649656/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=8f0c5975-65cf-4a02-baf0-786ba6649656",
        "durationSec": 3671,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:3c0fc244-3dc3-11f1-ab33-5f07c6c92de3",
        "startSec": 2950,
        "endSec": 3310,
        "why": "Now the lens widens to the whole Gospel. The speakers trace John’s opening days through the Cana wedding, explain the seven signs, and then identify the resurrection as an eighth sign: “New creation.” This shows why John links Genesis, the Torah’s eighth day, weddings, and resurrection across the book.",
        "episodeTitle": "Gospel of John Overview: A Unique Eyewitness",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG5222140559.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3787,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:db54663c-6a8e-11f1-8d65-4723e9decce5",
        "startSec": 2240,
        "endSec": 2420,
        "why": "This makes the Genesis pattern concrete in John 4. The speakers describe Jesus’ living water as moving people from “the death world you’re in of Genesis 1” into the “life and Eden world of Genesis 2,” then say, “I am the temple. I am the water of life. I am the spirit.” It shows new creation as God’s life reaching compromised and wounded people.",
        "episodeTitle": "John Overview: Living Water",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG6305125345.mp3",
        "durationSec": 4717,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:d2454984-com-96e88155-08af-3240-94ab-4e13ecb0d2e9",
        "startSec": 780,
        "endSec": 1090,
        "why": "The tour lands at John 20, where the pattern becomes resurrection and restored humanity. The clip keeps the “first day of the week” in view, has Mary mistake Jesus for “the gardener,” and explains that Jesus’ breathing on the disciples recalls Genesis 2:7: “humanity restored” and “life reborn.”",
        "episodeTitle": "John 20:1-31",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/z8yyjpv9fmtit6hd/john_201-3185o5y.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1726,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:beginning",
        "startSec": 2720,
        "endSec": 2900,
        "why": "This final perspective names the conflict underneath John’s creation language. The hosts describe darkness as the “anti-creation forces” dragging humanity toward disorder, then connect the resurrection with the claim that “the darkness couldn’t overcome the light.” It gives the preceding clips their final horizon: new creation is the triumph and continuation of God’s ordering, life-giving work.",
        "episodeTitle": "In the Beginning – John 1",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/8f0c5975-65cf-4a02-baf0-786ba6649656/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=8f0c5975-65cf-4a02-baf0-786ba6649656",
        "durationSec": 3671,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these teachers present John’s new creation as Genesis being reopened and carried forward in Jesus: the Word brings light, the new Adam brings life, water and Spirit make an Eden-like people, and the resurrection begins the eighth day. John’s allusions are not decorative references; they are the way he tells you what Jesus is doing and what his followers are becoming.",
